  "worked_solution": "To determine whether each sequence is an arithmetic sequence, we need to look at the jumps between terms in the sequence. If the jumps are the same, then they indeed represent a common difference of an arithmetic sequence.\n\n- In sequence I, the jumps are not all the same. Therefore, sequence I is not an arithmetic sequence.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14261220/assets/807549580ce3c6d83ac3a0e6b53e127d60197ec16ccf99fd1499928c0437882c.png)\n\n- In sequence II, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence II is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14261220/assets/77ce359fc2a02a86db5aafc4b665b14edd9425142ab4e77df6ced0880489d56b.png)\n\n- In sequence III, the jumps are not all the same. Therefore, sequence III is not an arithmetic sequence either.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14261220/assets/059f0c7009a43990d4b742ddc8b57b357fa2cd23b037ad5136f3121f2c62a036.png)\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence II is an arithmetic sequence.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
